"""
What {{.ProjectName}} DEPLOYS — the workload declarations.

YOURS. forge scaffolds this file once and never rewrites it. `forge
scaffold service|worker|binary|operator` APPENDS a new declaration to the
end; nothing else forge does touches what is already here. Edit it
freely — reorder it, comment it, delete a workload you no longer ship.

WHAT A WORKLOAD IS
==================
One deployable unit: an executable, the args that select what it does,
and where it runs. In Kubernetes terms, what becomes ONE Deployment.

A workload is NOT a proto service. One binary can mount a dozen Connect
services on a shared mux and still be ONE workload, because it is one
process with one image and one set of args. Declaring a workload per
proto service renders N Deployments for what is really one process —
that is the single most expensive mistake this file's vocabulary
exists to prevent.

  kind = "service"   Deployment + Service (named ports)
  kind = "worker"    Deployment, no Service (nothing dials it)
  kind = "cron"      CronJob on `schedule`
  kind = "job"       one-shot; `before` names who waits on it
  kind = "operator"  manager Deployment + cluster RBAC
  kind = "tool"      built into the image, never scheduled

WHAT EACH KIND CAN EXPRESS
==========================
A kind does not merely select an expansion; it decides which fields are
MEANINGFUL. `forge.workloads` declares that as data — the kind ×
capability matrix in the forge module's `workloads/capabilities.k` — and
both the schema checks and the renderers read the same table.

The practical consequence: a field your kind cannot express is REJECTED
at render time, naming the field and the kind, rather than being
silently dropped. `replicas` on a cron fails (a CronJob's concurrency is
concurrencyPolicy, not a replica count); `cluster_rbac` on a worker fails
and points you at `namespaced_rbac`, or at declaring the workload an
operator if it genuinely watches cluster-wide.

RBAC is the capability most often needed and most often forgotten. Any
scheduled kind can ask for in-namespace permission:

    reaper = fw.Workload {
        name = "reaper"
        kind = "worker"
        namespaced_rbac = [
            {apiGroups = ["example.com"], resources = ["widgets"], verbs = ["list", "watch"]}
        ]
    }

Declare nothing and you get a ServiceAccount with no Role and no mounted
token — the right default, because most workloads never call the
Kubernetes API and a blanket Role would hand an RCE a credential dump.
An `operator` uses `cluster_rbac` instead; the two tiers are exclusive.

DECLARE COMPLETE, REFINE PER ENV
================================
Each declaration below is a COMPLETE, valid workload — ports and all —
not a fragment awaiting assembly. That is what makes it readable on its
own: this file answers "what is this thing?" in full.

An environment then REFINES what it needs with KCL's `|` merge operator,
in `deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k`:

    _api = wl.{{.PrimaryIdent}} | {replicas = 3}

Only the difference is stated, the rest is inherited, and because `|`
preserves the schema type, every `check:` in forge.workloads re-runs on
the merged result. An env that sets `replicas = 0` is rejected AT THAT
ENV, naming the field — a base declaration cannot be refined into an
invalid one.

ADDING SOMETHING THAT ISN'T GO CODE
===================================
Anything can be a workload, whether or not forge generated it. Declare
it in the env that HAS it rather than here, and only that env gets it:

    # in deploy/kcl/dev/main.k
    _nats = fw.Workload {
        name = "nats"
        kind = "service"
        image = "nats"
        registry = "docker.io/library"
        ports = [fw.Port {name = "client", port = 4222, expose = True}]
    }
    _workloads = [_api, _nats]

That is how you run a containerized database locally while prod points
at a hosted one.
"""

import forge.workloads as fw

# One NAMED declaration per deployable unit. The name is what each
# environment refines (`wl.{{.PrimaryIdent}} | {...}`), which is why these are
# bindings rather than entries in one anonymous list.
{{- if .Workloads}}

{{.Workloads}}
{{- else}}

# No workloads yet. `forge scaffold service <name>` appends the first
# one — or write it by hand:
#
#     billing = fw.Workload {
#         name = "billing"
#         kind = "service"
#         ports = [fw.Port {name = "http", port = 8080, expose = True}]
#         build = {type = "go", cmd = "./cmd/{{.ProjectName}}", output_name = "{{.ProjectName}}"}
#     }
{{- end}}

# Every workload this project ships. `forge scaffold` appends to this
# list, and each environment imports it as its starting set.
#
# An environment is NOT obliged to deploy all of them — `deploy/kcl/<env>/main.k`
# names what IT runs, so an env can drop one, refine one, or add a workload
# only it has. This list is "what exists", not "what every env deploys".
ALL: [fw.Workload] = [{{.WorkloadIdents}}]
